    "question": "McKenna has 34 stuffed animals. Kenley has twice as many as McKenna.  Tenly has 5 more than Kenley . How many stuffed animals do the three girls have in all?",
    "gold": "175",
    "answer": "Kenley has 2 * 34 stuffed animals = <<2*34=68>>68 stuffed animals.\nTenly has 68 stuffed animals + 5 = <<68+5=73>>73 stuffed animals.\nThe three girls total had 34 + 68 + 73 = <<34+68+73=175>>175 stuffed animals\n#### 175"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine the total number of stuffed animals the three girls have, we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the number of stuffed animals Kenley has:**\n   Kenley has twice as many stuffed animals as McKenna.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of stuffed animals Kenley has} = 2 \\times \\text{Number of stuffed animals McKenna has}\n   \\]\n   McKenna has 34 stuffed animals.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of stuffed animals Kenley has} = 2 \\times 34 = 68\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the number of stuffed animals Tenly has:**\n   Tenly has 5 more stuffed animals than Kenley.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of stuffed animals Tenly has} = \\text{Number of stuffed animals Kenley has} + 5\n   \\]\n   Kenley has 68 stuffed animals.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of stuffed animals Tenly has} = 68 + 5 = 73\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total number of stuffed animals:**\n   Add the number of stuffed animals each girl has.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of stuffed animals} = \\text{Number of stuffed animals McKenna has} + \\text{Number of stuffed animals Kenley has} + \\text{Number of stuffed animals Tenly has}\n   \\]\n   McKenna has 34, Kenley has 68, and Tenly has 73.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of stuffed animals} = 34 + 68 + 73\n   \\]\n\n4. **Perform the addition:**\n   \\[\n   34 + 68 = 102\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   102 + 73 = 175\n   \\]\n\nThus, the total number of stuffed animals the three girls have is \\(\\boxed{175}\\).",
